    <body>
      <sec id="sec1">
        <label>1</label>
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>The exponential growth of biomedical literature has created an urgent need for automated text mining tools. PubMed currently indexes over 35 million citations, while PMC provides full-text access to more than 8 million articles.</p>
        <p>XML parsing is a critical component of biomedical informatics pipelines. This study presents a comprehensive framework for parsing both PubMed and PMC XML formats with high accuracy and efficiency.</p>
        <sec id="sec1-1">
          <label>1.1</label>
          <title>Motivation</title>
          <p>Traditional XML parsers often struggle with the complexity and scale of biomedical literature databases. Our approach addresses these challenges through streaming architecture and DTD-compliant parsing.</p>
        </sec>
      </sec>
      <sec id="sec2">
        <label>2</label>
        <title>Methods</title>
        <p>We implemented a StAX-based parser that processes XML documents in a memory-efficient manner.</p>
        <sec id="sec2-1">
          <label>2.1</label>
          <title>Architecture</title>
          <p>The parser architecture consists of three layers: input processing, element parsing, and data validation.</p>
        </sec>
        <sec id="sec2-2">
          <label>2.2</label>
          <title>Implementation</title>
          <p>Java 17 with Lombok annotations was used to minimize boilerplate code while maintaining type safety.</p>
        </sec>
      </sec>
      <sec id="sec3">
        <label>3</label>
        <title>Results</title>
        <p>Our evaluation demonstrated superior performance across multiple metrics.</p>
        <fig id="fig1">
          <label>Figure 1</label>
          <caption>
            <title>Parser Performance Comparison</title>
            <p>Comparison of parsing speed across different XML parsers.</p>
          </caption>
